C = [
("h1", "9. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "Claim: the volume’s reading, that the prophets condemn the worshipers and the way they worship and never the worship the LORD commanded, answers the objections best made against it, and where an objector is right the volume says so. Modern writers who support parts of the argument are named beside those who press against it. A writer is quoted only where the author has read the words in a free text. Where a position is known to the author only because another writer or an abstract reports it, the volume says so, names the report, and puts no quotation marks around the position."),
("sub", "9.1 Who agrees, and where the volume differs"),
("p", "Four modern writers hold, in their own ways, that the prophets did not reject sacrifice as such. Göran Eidevall, who teaches Old Testament at Uppsala, writes in an article of 2018 that his study “refutes the view that prophets like Amos and Jeremiah rejected all sacrifices” (//Religions// 9, no. 12, article titled //Reciprocity and the Risk of Rejection//). He reads their cult-critical oracles as bound to particular situations. In a short piece of 2017 for Yale University Press he calls the opposite picture of the prophets “anachronistic.” Blessing Boloje, writing on Micah 6:6–8 in //Old Testament Essays// in 2019, concludes that “Micah’s message is not a rejection of ritual but maintaining the right priorities.” Alphonso Groenewald, in //HTS Teologiese Studies// in 2019, reads Amos 5:21–24 and Isaiah 1:10–20 in the same direction, and quotes another scholar’s sentence that “The problem indeed is the lack of social ethics of the community of the faithful and not with the rituals themselves.” Matt Lynch, in a blog post of 2017 on the WTC Theology blog, writes that “It’s important to remember that the prophets were rhetoricians” and he notes that Jeremiah himself says sacrifices will be restored (Jeremiah 33:18)."),
("p", "The volume agrees with them on the conclusion and differs on the method. Eidevall and Lynch read the strong sentences as situational or rhetorical: said for an occasion, or to provoke. The volume reads them, wherever the wording allows, as the Hebrew idiom of rank, this more than that, and finds in each the same two things: the offerings are named as the LORD’s own appointment, and the charge falls on the worshiper. That is a narrower claim than rhetoric, and a reader who prefers the rhetorical reading loses nothing in the conclusion."),
("sub", "9.2 Objection: the prophets rejected sacrifice itself"),
("p", "This is the strongest opposing position, and it has a long history in the study of the prophets. John Barton, in //The Prophets and the Cult// (in //Temple and Worship in Biblical Israel//, edited by John Day, 2007), holds, according to an abstract of the chapter published by the Temple Studies Group, that a handful of passages, among them Micah 6:6, Amos 5:25, Jeremiah 2:1–3, Jeremiah 7:22 and Psalm 50, are hostile to sacrificial activity in itself and not only to sacrifice offered by the unrepentant. The volume knows Barton’s chapter only through that abstract and describes it in its own words. Eidevall reports that Barton also describes Amos as an anti-ritualist (in //The Theology of the Book of Amos//, 2012), and he reports that Paul Volz, in 1937, argued from five observations that Amos and Jeremiah wanted the cult abolished. Groenewald reports that William McKane (1982) and Ronald Hendel (1995) read the prophets as setting the ethical against the ritual. The volume has read none of these works; it describes the positions as these two writers report them."),
("p", "Reply. Barton’s five passages are each read in the chapters before this one, and the reply is the reading. Micah 6:6–8 is a series of questions put by the worshiper, answered by “what doth the LORD require of thee” (Micah 6:8), with no sentence abolishing the offerings (chapter 4). Amos 5:25 is a question that is itself contested (chapter 3). Jeremiah 7:22 is read against Exodus 19:5, Jeremiah 7:23 and Jeremiah 33:18 (chapter 5). Jeremiah 2:1–3 praises Israel’s love in the wilderness and calls Israel “holiness unto the LORD, and the firstfruits of his increase”; it speaks of Israel herself as the firstfruits and mentions no offering of the worshipers that the LORD refuses. Psalm 50 says, “I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices or thy burnt offerings, to have been continually before me” (Psalm 50:8), and then says what he does not need and what he asks (chapter 1). The volume grants that the sentences read alone can sound like abolition. It holds that they do not read so in their contexts."),
("p", "Volz’s five observations, as Eidevall reports them, can each be met from the text. That the critique reached the central sanctuaries is true of Amos’s Bethel, which was the king’s own chapel (Amos 7:13), and Eidevall notes that Amos never charges the Jerusalem cult; Jeremiah’s charge falls on those who trust the house, not on the house’s offerings (chapter 5). That the prophets proposed no reform fits a message that said the day of judgment was fixed; but Jeremiah 7:3 sets the condition, “Amend your ways and your doings, and I will cause you to dwell in this place,” and Isaiah 1:16–20 offers the same. That they idealized the past is what Jeremiah 7:22, 23 and Amos 5:25 are said to show, and chapters 3 and 5 read both. That priests persecuted them is true, but the charge against Amos was conspiracy and the foretelling of the king’s death and the people’s exile (Amos 7:10, 11), not the abolition of offerings. That some oracles imply total rejection is the very point at issue, and the chapters read each of them."),
("sub", "9.3 Objection: ritual has little value; ethics is the whole matter"),
("p", "A second objection grants that the prophets did not abolish the cult and holds that they saw little worth in it. Boloje and Groenewald, in //Scriptura// in 2015, propose that “these prophets could not see the importance of rituals for the improvement of ethical life,” and say this criticism “lies at the heart of the controversy between the prophets and the priest.” They add that the cult was acceptable where it served ethical living. Groenewald, in 2019, reports that Hendel holds the classical prophets contrasted the ethical with the ritual. The objection is that the prophets valued the offerings so little that the volume’s emphasis on what they left standing makes too much of it."),
("p", "Reply. The volume grants that the prophets demanded conduct and that offerings without it are refused: Boloje himself says rituals without ethical behaviour are worthless, and the volume has said the same of Isaiah 1, Amos 5 and Jeremiah 7. But the prophets do not say the offerings are of little value. They call them “mine offerings” (Hosea 8:13), they say the LORD will “require” them in the land (Ezekiel 20:40), and Malachi charges the priests for offering what a governor would refuse, which treats the offering as a matter of weight (Malachi 1:8). A man who thinks an offering of little value does not object to a blind lamb. The charge is that the offering was brought by the unrepentant and brought in a state that dishonored the one it was brought to. That makes them of great value, not little."),
("sub", "9.4 Objection: Ezekiel 20:25–26 says the LORD gave bad laws"),
("p", "The hardest text comes from a prophet. “Wherefore I gave them also statutes that were not good, and judgments whereby they should not live; and I polluted them in their own gifts, in that they caused to pass through the fire all that openeth the womb, that I might make them desolate, to the end that they might know that I am the LORD” (Ezekiel 20:25, 26). Hyam Maccoby, in an article in the //Journal of Textual Reasoning// in 1999, reports that modern Jewish commentators led by Moshe Greenberg have taken the verses to say that Ezekiel accuses God of giving bad laws, among them a law of firstborn sacrifice. Maccoby rejects that. “The notion of a God who deliberately gives bad laws is surely nonsensical,” he writes, and he reads the verses as Ezekiel’s report of what the rebels said: “Ezekiel represents the rebels as attributing the new teaching to Yahweh himself.”"),
("p", "Reply. The volume and Maccoby differ on how the verses work and agree on what they cannot do. Maccoby takes the statutes that were not good as the rebels’ claim; the volume reads them as a judicial giving-over, in the order of the chapter. The statutes by which a man lives are the ones given first, and Israel “walked not in my statutes” (Ezekiel 20:11, 13, 21). Then comes “Wherefore I gave them also statutes that were not good” (Ezekiel 20:25), a consequence of the refusal. The next verse names the practice, the burning of the firstborn, and Jeremiah 7:31 says of the burning of children that the LORD “commanded them not, neither came it into my heart.” Whichever reading is taken, the verses are not a repudiation of the offerings, because the same chapter promises that in his holy mountain “there will I require your offerings, and the firstfruits of your oblations, with all your holy things” (Ezekiel 20:40). The volume does not rest its case on choosing between Maccoby’s reading and its own."),
("sub", "9.5 Objection: Hebrews 10 shows the prophets were right to reject sacrifice"),
("p", "The objection takes Hebrews 10:8, 9 as a ruling that the offerings were taken away, and concludes that the prophets were announcing their end. No writer is named for it; it is stated in the author’s words. Reply. Chapter 8 read the passage. The letter says the Law’s sacrifices could not take away sins and that Christ’s offering does. It does not say the prophets abolished the offerings, and it does not say their charges were charges against the offerings. What it shares with the prophets is a verdict about their power, and what it adds is a person."),
("sub", "9.6 Objection: are the churches’ rites condemned too?"),
("p", "The objection asks whether those who keep a rite today stand under the prophets’ charge. No writer is named for it. Reply. The volume does not say so. The prophets’ charges are brought against Israel’s worship, in Israel’s land, at the house the LORD appointed. Nothing in the chapters was a rule for the Body, and nothing was a charge against it. The volume holds that the Body is in no Israelite covenant and states no critique of Christian ritual. A reader who wants to ask what the prophets’ pattern shows about any worship is free to ask it, and the volume has not answered."),
("ctbl", "The objections and the replies in one table.", [
    ("Objection", "Held by", "Reply in one line"),
    ("The prophets rejected sacrifice itself", "Barton (2007), as an abstract of his chapter reports; Volz, McKane and Hendel, as Eidevall and Groenewald report", "Each of Barton’s passages is read in its context; none abolishes the offerings"),
    ("Ritual has little value; ethics is the matter", "Boloje and Groenewald (2015); Hendel, as Groenewald reports", "The offerings are “mine offerings” and are “required”; the charge is the worshiper, not the worth"),
    ("Ezekiel 20:25–26 says the LORD gave bad laws", "Greenberg and others, as Maccoby reports; Maccoby opposes", "Not a repudiation of the offerings; the chapter promises them (Ezekiel 20:40)"),
    ("Hebrews 10 shows the prophets were right to reject sacrifice", "none named", "The letter’s verdict is about their power; the prophets are not made to abolish them"),
    ("The churches’ rites are condemned too", "none named", "Not said; the charges are Israel’s and nothing here is a rule for the Body"),
  ], [0.3, 0.3, 0.4]),
("sub", "9.7 What the volume takes from the replies"),
("bul", [
    "Eidevall, Boloje, Groenewald and Lynch agree that the prophets did not reject sacrifice as such; the volume differs from the first and last in reading the sentences as rank rather than situation or rhetoric.",
    "The strongest opposing voice, Barton, is answered passage by passage; the volume knows his chapter only through an abstract and says so.",
    "The volume grants that the prophets demanded conduct and refused offerings without it, and holds that they never treated the offerings as of little worth.",
    "Ezekiel 20:25, 26 is read in the chapter’s order, with its closing promise of the offerings; the volume differs from Maccoby on how the verses work and agrees with him that they do not repudiate the offerings.",
    "The volume states no critique of Christian ritual and assigns the prophets’ charges to the worship they were brought against.",
  ]),
]
